EXTRA = [
    {"after": "special", "id": "pediatric", "nav": "Children in depth", "tag": "Special populations",
     "h2": "Children and young people: growth, family, and the road to adult care",
     "html": """
<p>A child with chronic kidney disease (CKD) is not a small adult. Three things are moving at once: a body that is growing, a mind that is developing, and a family that is carrying the treatment. Every decision you make for a child has to fit all three. The earlier section introduced growth, vein protection, needle fear, school, and transition. This section explains why children differ, and what that means at the bedside and the chair.</p>
<p><strong>Different causes, different fluid rules.</strong> In adults, CKD is driven mostly by diabetes and high blood pressure (BP). In children, the most common cause is congenital anomalies of the kidneys and urinary tract, followed by glomerular and inherited diseases. Many children with malformed or underdeveloped kidneys cannot concentrate their urine or hold on to sodium. They pass large volumes of dilute urine and lose salt every day. The adult reflex to restrict fluid can dehydrate them, and they may need salt and water supplements as prescribed. A bout of vomiting or diarrhea can quickly reduce their kidney function further. Ask, for every child: how much urine does this child pass, and what fluid and salt plan has the pediatric nephrologist set? Restriction is individual, never assumed.</p>
<p><strong>Body size changes the arithmetic.</strong> A small child&rsquo;s whole blood volume is less than a liter. Every quantity that is routine in an adult becomes large in proportion:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fluid gain.</strong> Half a kilogram gained between sessions is about 5% of the body weight of a 10-kg child, the same proportion as 3.5 kg in a 70-kg adult. Fluid status is therefore judged as a percentage of body weight, on the same scale each time, with the same clothing or a weighed diaper.</li>
<li><strong>The blood circuit.</strong> Blood held in the lines and dialyzer is a much larger share of a child&rsquo;s circulation, so circuit size, priming, and the rate of fluid removal are chosen by the pediatric team. A child can become hypotensive quickly and may show it late: restlessness, pallor, yawning, vomiting, or a rising heart rate often come before the BP falls.</li>
<li><strong>Heat and glucose.</strong> Infants lose heat to an extracorporeal circuit and to cool rooms, and have small glucose reserves. Temperature and, when ordered, blood glucose are checked during treatment.</li>
<li><strong>Blood sampling.</strong> Repeated samples are a real blood loss for a small child and add to anemia. Draws are coordinated so that one sample serves several tests.</li>
<li><strong>BP.</strong> Normal values depend on age, sex, and height, so readings are interpreted against pediatric reference tables, not adult thresholds. A cuff that is too small reads falsely high; the bladder of the cuff must fit the arm.</li>
<li><strong>Medicines.</strong> Doses are calculated by weight or body surface area. Calculations are independently double-checked according to hospital policy, and liquid preparations and their measuring devices are checked with the parent before discharge.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Choice of dialysis.</strong> Peritoneal dialysis (PD) is often chosen for infants and young children. It is done at home, needs no needles or large-vessel access, and, when a machine runs it overnight, leaves the day free for school. Hemodialysis (HD) in a child usually depends on a central venous catheter until a fistula can be made, which is why protecting veins from the first contact matters so much.[[shroff2019]] For most children, either is a bridge to transplantation. Prescriptions and settings for children belong to pediatric nephrology teams. An adult unit that receives a child, in an emergency or a disaster, contacts a pediatric nephrologist before treatment.</p>
<p><strong>Development: the same care, delivered differently.</strong></p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What the child is working on</th><th>How the nurse adapts</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Infant and toddler</td><td>Feeding, attachment to parents, fear of strangers</td><td>Keep a parent present and holding where possible; protect feeding routines, which are themselves treatment; involve the dietitian early when feeding is poor or tube feeding is planned</td></tr>
<tr><td>Preschool</td><td>Magical thinking; may believe procedures are punishment for being bad</td><td>Short, honest explanations immediately before a procedure; say plainly that it is not a punishment; offer small choices that are real (which arm, which sticker)</td></tr>
<tr><td>School age</td><td>Understanding rules and how things work; belonging with classmates</td><td>Explain the body and the machine with drawings; involve the child in simple tasks such as reading the scale; plan around school</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adolescent</td><td>Independence, privacy, identity, body image, relationships, risk-taking</td><td>See the young person alone for part of each visit; respect confidentiality within the limits of safety; talk about sexual health, contraception, alcohol, and smoking without judgment; involve them in decisions</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Family-centered care.</strong> The family is the unit of care. Parents know the child best, and they are partners. Teach and assess them to the same return-demonstration standard as any home operator. Kidney failure in a child often means a parent stops working, the family travels far to a pediatric center, and siblings get less attention. In many Filipino families grandparents and other relatives share the care. Include them in teaching when they will give medicines or do exchanges. Ask each parent, separately where possible, how they are managing. Refer to social work and psychological support early, not only in crisis.</p>
<p><strong>School.</strong> Schooling is a treatment goal, because education shapes the life the child will lead as an adult. With the family&rsquo;s consent, you can prepare a short written plan for the school. It covers the condition in plain words, the access and how to protect it, the fluid and food limits that apply at school, medicines due in school hours, and what to do and whom to call in an emergency. Plan absences for dialysis and clinic visits with teachers, so schoolwork can be sent home. Encourage play and physical activity, within any limits on contact sports the team sets.</p>
<p><strong>Transition to adult care.</strong> The transfer from a pediatric to an adult service is a recognized period of risk. Appointments are missed, medicines are skipped, and transplants are lost. An international consensus statement describes transition as a planned process, individualized for each young person, focused on self-management skills and on the support around them, and involving both teams.[[watson2011]] It is a consensus statement, not a tested program. In practice you help by:</p>
<ul>
<li>starting early, in the early teenage years, with small steps of self-management;</li>
<li>using a readiness checklist the young person can see: names their condition and each medicine and its purpose, knows what to do for a missed dose, refills prescriptions, books and attends appointments, and knows when and how to call for help;</li>
<li>arranging for the adult team to meet the young person before transfer, ideally in a joint visit;</li>
<li>sending a written summary that includes not only the medical history but also what motivates the young person and what has worked for adherence;</li>
<li>making sure the first adult appointment is booked before the last pediatric one, and following up anyone who does not attend.</li>
</ul>
<p>Transfer is often abrupt at a fixed age, and adult units may have no young-adult clinic. If you notice a newly transferred 18-year-old in an adult unit, you can make a large difference simply by giving them time, privacy, and a named person to contact.</p>
<p><strong>For every child, ask three things before any order is carried out: what does this child weigh today, how much urine does the child pass, and who at home is doing the care?</strong></p>
"""},
    {"after": "pediatric", "id": "pregnancy", "nav": "Pregnancy in depth", "tag": "Special populations",
     "h2": "Pregnancy in kidney disease, on dialysis, and after transplant",
     "html": """
<p>Picture the pregnancy as a second household moving into the mother&rsquo;s body. It brings its own demands for water, salt, and blood flow, and the mother&rsquo;s kidneys must supply them. The picture has a limit. The placenta is not a quiet tenant. It can also send out signals that injure the mother&rsquo;s own blood vessels.</p>
<p><strong>Why pregnancy strains the kidney.</strong> In a healthy pregnancy, blood volume expands, kidney blood flow and filtration rise substantially, and BP normally falls in mid-pregnancy as vessels relax. A kidney with reduced reserve may not rise to this. Creatinine climbs, protein in the urine increases, and BP becomes harder to control. Preeclampsia adds a second problem. It begins with poor development of the placenta&rsquo;s blood supply. The placenta then releases substances that injure the lining of blood vessels throughout the mother&rsquo;s body. The results are high BP, protein in the urine, and damage to the kidneys, liver, brain, and clotting system. Women with CKD, whose vessels are already damaged, are more likely to develop it.</p>
<p><strong>Three situations, three sets of risks.</strong> Outcomes come mostly from observational studies, so the risks below are associations.[[wiles2019]]</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Risks associated with it</th><th>Nursing focus</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>CKD, not on dialysis</td><td>Preeclampsia, preterm birth, a small baby, and a loss of kidney function that may not recover after delivery; risks are higher with lower kidney function, more protein in the urine, and high BP before pregnancy</td><td>Ask about pregnancy plans and contraception at review; ensure early referral to nephrology and a high-risk obstetric service; BP and urine protein checked as scheduled</td></tr>
<tr><td>On dialysis</td><td>Reduced fertility but pregnancy still occurs, often recognized late; high rates of preterm birth, excess amniotic fluid, a small baby, and preeclampsia</td><td>Consider pregnancy in a woman of reproductive age with nausea, missed periods, or unexplained weight gain, and report so a test is arranged; intensified dialysis as below</td></tr>
<tr><td>After transplant</td><td>Generally better outcomes than on dialysis, but higher rates of preeclampsia and preterm birth than in the general population; risks are higher if graft function is poor or BP uncontrolled</td><td>Planned pregnancy with stable function and safe medicines; drug levels and graft function checked more often; urinary infection reported promptly</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Acute kidney injury (AKI) in pregnancy.</strong> Any pregnant woman, with or without CKD, can develop AKI from severe vomiting, heavy bleeding before or after delivery, infection, or preeclampsia. Watch urine output and fluid balance closely in these situations. Report a falling output. Do not explain it away.</p>
<p><strong>Intensified dialysis, in principle.</strong> When a woman on HD becomes pregnant, the dialysis plan changes substantially. The basis is observational. A Canadian cohort treated with intensive HD was compared with a United States registry. Live births were more frequent in the Canadian cohort, 86.4% against 61.4%. Live births tracked weekly dialysis hours: 48% in women dialyzed 20 hours a week or less, and 85% in those dialyzed more than 36 hours a week, with longer pregnancies and heavier babies at higher intensity.[[hladunewich2014]] The cohorts came from different countries and eras, so the result is an association, not proof of cause. A United Kingdom guideline recommends intensified HD for pregnant women already on dialysis, with care shared between nephrology and obstetrics.[[wiles2019]] The hours, frequency, and targets are the nephrologist&rsquo;s prescription. The nursing consequences are predictable:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>More sessions mean different chemistry.</strong> Near-daily dialysis can remove too much potassium and phosphate and can make the blood too alkaline, so the dialysate is often adjusted by order and blood tests are more frequent. Check that the current order is the one on the machine.</li>
<li><strong>Gentle fluid removal.</strong> The placenta has no ability to protect its own blood flow. A fall in the mother&rsquo;s BP during treatment reduces flow to the baby, so fluid removal is slow, target weight is reviewed often as the pregnancy adds real weight, and low BP is prevented and reported.</li>
<li><strong>Nutrition.</strong> Frequent dialysis removes water-soluble vitamins and amino acids, while the pregnancy needs more protein and folic acid. Diet is liberalized, and supplements are given as prescribed, with the dietitian involved.</li>
<li><strong>Anemia and anticoagulation.</strong> Anemia usually worsens, and doses of iron and erythropoiesis-stimulating agents typically rise by order. Anticoagulation for the circuit follows the order written for pregnancy.</li>
<li><strong>Fetal monitoring.</strong> Fetal heart checks around sessions and ultrasound scans follow the obstetric plan; the dialysis nurse knows the plan and whom to call.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a woman on PD, fill volumes usually need to fall as the uterus grows, and many programs consider a change to HD later in pregnancy. These decisions belong to the nephrologist.</p>
<p><strong>Medicines that must be reviewed before pregnancy.</strong> Some medicines common in kidney care can harm a developing baby. The safest time to change them is before conception, not after a positive test.[[wiles2019]]</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mycophenolate</strong>, used after transplant and for lupus and some glomerular diseases, is associated with miscarriage and birth defects and is replaced with a safer alternative before conception under specialist supervision.</li>
<li><strong>Renin-angiotensin blockers</strong>, meaning angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors such as enalapril and angiotensin receptor blockers such as losartan, protect the kidneys in CKD, but exposure in later pregnancy may impair the baby&rsquo;s kidney development and reduce amniotic fluid. When and how they are stopped or replaced is the physician&rsquo;s decision, made as part of pregnancy planning.</li>
<li><strong>Other agents</strong> used in transplant and CKD, including sirolimus and everolimus, are also reviewed, as the earlier section described.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your part is to find these women and refer them. At medicine reconciliation, notice every woman of reproductive age taking these medicines. Ask whether she is planning a pregnancy or could become pregnant, and refer her to the nephrologist or transplant team for preconception planning. A woman who finds she is pregnant while taking one of them contacts the team that day and does not stop any medicine on her own. Stopping an immunosuppressant or a BP medicine abruptly carries its own danger.</p>
<p><strong>Preeclampsia: signs that need immediate reporting.</strong> In a woman with CKD, BP and urine protein may already be abnormal, so preeclampsia is harder to recognize. That is a reason for a lower threshold to report, not a higher one. After 20 weeks of pregnancy, report immediately to both the obstetric and kidney teams:</p>
<ul>
<li>BP higher than her usual readings, measured with a correctly sized cuff, especially if rising across sessions or visits;</li>
<li>a severe or persistent headache, blurred vision, flashing lights, or spots before the eyes;</li>
<li>pain below the ribs on the right side or in the upper abdomen, often with nausea or vomiting;</li>
<li>sudden swelling of the face or hands, or rapid weight gain not explained by fluid intake;</li>
<li>breathlessness, or a fall in urine output in a woman who still passes urine;</li>
<li>reduced fetal movement, vaginal bleeding, or contractions.</li>
</ul>
<p>A seizure in a pregnant or recently delivered woman is eclampsia until proven otherwise, and it is an emergency. The physician interprets laboratory changes such as falling platelets, rising liver enzymes, or a rapid rise in creatinine. Preeclampsia can also appear or worsen in the days after delivery, so teach these warning signs again at discharge.</p>
<p><strong>After delivery.</strong> Blood volume and kidney function shift quickly, so fluid targets, dialysis prescriptions, and drug levels are reviewed again. Medicines are checked for safety in breastfeeding before they are restarted or changed. Ask about mood. Postpartum depression is common, and a newborn&rsquo;s demands add to those of kidney care.</p>
<p><strong>Contraception counseling is part of kidney care.</strong> Ask every woman of reproductive age with CKD, on dialysis, or after transplant about her plans, and offer referral for contraception counseling. Fertility often returns within months of a successful transplant. An unplanned pregnancy on mycophenolate, or with poorly controlled disease, is what planning prevents. The method belongs to the physician or family-planning service, because some methods are unsuitable with high BP or a history of blood clots. Your role is to raise the question without judgment, record the answer, and make the referral. In the Philippines, maternal-fetal medicine specialists are concentrated in large cities, so early referral also gives the family time to plan travel and costs.</p>
<p><strong>In a pregnant woman with kidney disease, report early and report to both teams. A rising BP with a headache is preeclampsia until someone has examined her.</strong></p>
"""},
]
